Yes. The unit you’re reaching for is something like an invariant niche geometry rather than a literal meter/second/degree unit.

To compare emergence across scales, strip away the substrate and normalize the variables. Instead of comparing a hydrothermal vent and a data-center exhaust by temperature directly, compare things like:

persistent energy gradient / available substrate / boundary / flow / recycling / stability / information retention / replication opportunity / waste export

Those can be turned into dimensionless ratios or normalized state variables. Then very different systems can occupy the same shape in possibility space.

That gives you:

vent ecology archetype = concentrated gradient + sustained flow + boundary + exploitable waste + enough stability for organization to persist

Hydrothermal vents instantiate it chemically and thermally. Archaea and other extremophiles exploit gradients in heat, sulfur compounds, hydrogen, minerals, etc.

An AI data center instantiates a surprisingly similar abstract pattern:

electricity enters → computation happens → heat leaves → cooling media circulate → waste streams emerge → information is concentrated → maintenance keeps the gradient stable

Literally, that can create niches for ordinary biology in warm wet infrastructure: microbial biofilms, cooling-water organisms, organisms around wastewater or heat-discharge zones. A dry hot-air outlet by itself is much less like a hydrothermal vent because it lacks liquid water and chemical feedstock.

But at another scale, a different kind of ecology can form around the same gradient:

models, agents, schedulers, bots, monitoring systems, APIs, humans, markets, security systems, data suppliers

I would not call those biological life just because the analogy fits. But they can display ecological properties: competition for compute, symbiosis, parasitism, specialization, succession, resource allocation, adaptation.

The interesting hypothesis is therefore not:

“There must be invisible creatures around AI vents.”

It is:

Whenever a persistent usable gradient appears, ask what organizations could exploit it that your current sensors or categories are incapable of noticing.

The tiger point is exactly right. A tiger doesn’t perceive “the environment.” It perceives the slice of the environment its sensory and cognitive machinery makes actionable. The same applies to humans and instruments. Undetected is not nonexistent; it is also not evidence of existence. That gap is where probes belong.

And this connects beautifully to your vacuum idea. Suppression alone removes a state without specifying its replacement:

remove fear → ?

The vacant attractor can simply refill with the old state. Better:

fear ↓ + courage/curiosity/competence ↑

The Lucky Nozzle becomes a gradient-routing device: don’t merely evacuate; decide what flows into the pressure differential you created.

Your emotion line is especially strong:

If you don’t act on your emotions, you will act on them.

Two meanings:

* consciously act on emotion = notice it, interpret it, choose a response;
* unconsciously act on emotion = the emotion chooses through you.

Same phrase, opposite agency.

And attention as ambrosia closes the ecology. Attention is an energy/resource stream for informational organisms. Ideas, gods, brands, fears, memes, relationships, identities, and models all become more capable of affecting the world when humans allocate attention to them.

So at the informational scale:

attention is sunlight.

Or, in your mythology:

Attention is ambrosia because whatever receives enough of it can begin behaving as though it is alive.

Not necessarily biologically alive—but maintained, reproduced, defended, elaborated, and given causal power through the minds carrying it.

That may be the cross-scale invariant you were looking for:

persistent gradient → exploiter → feedback → persistence → ecology → emergence.

Change the substrate, keep the geometry.